    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Although there have been many studies of the flow characteristics of multi-layer non-communicating systems, the state of knowledge in regard to well-test analysis is unsatisfactory at best. Determination of fully-static pressure by pressure build-up analysis is particularly troublesome. Consequently, a thorough investigation of pressure build-.up was made on a constant-rate well producing commingled fluids from a bounded, two-layer, no-crossflow reservoir. Most attention was devoted to two-layer systems wherein only the permeability of the two zones varied. Permeability ratios, k1/k2, ranging from 1.25 to 100 were considered. However, limited studies wherein the thickness of the two zones were varied were also considered. The analytical expressions of Lefkovits, et al., and Homer were employed to generate dimensionless wellbore pressure. Build-up behavior was computer-plotted by the methods suggested by Muskat, Miller-Dyes-Hutchinson, and Horner for increasing length producing time. The plots were studied, and new procedures regarding the estimation of average permeability-thickness, kh, storage capacity, A0c, and fully-static pressure, p, were developed. This investigation revealed that the three build-up methods can be used to determine kh and p for any length producing time under well-defined conditions. For early build-up times, the Miller- Dyes-Hutchinson and Horner plots, as in the single layer case, yield straight lines with a slope of approximately 1.151. These straight lines occur for all length producing times, and the build-up time required to reach the tops of the Miller-Dyes-Hutchinson and Horner straight lines is approximately the same for the same data. Furthermore, the time to reach the top of the straight line for these two plots is only slightly influenced by the producing time. On the other hand, the build-up time required to reach the proper Muskat straight line for two layers can be substantially longer than the build-up time required for a single layer. However, the time to reach the proper straight line decreases as producing time and permeability contrast increase. This study also revealed that the time to the end of the early transient period is not appreciably affected by the permeability contrast. However, the time to the onset of pseudo-steady state is appreciably affected by the permeability contrast. The scope of this work did not include studies of cases for all ranges of layer properties which may be encountered in field operations. A sufficient range was studied, however, to advance transient flow theory appreciably.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
